  factId: fact_sun_seagrass_bed
  habitatName: Seagrass Bed
  category: Marine Angiosperm Meadow
  zoneIndex: 0
  depthRangeText: "5\u201340 m"
  habitatDescription: Vast submerged meadows formed by marine angiosperms (true flowering
    plants) anchored into soft sands and silts in clear, sunlit coastal waters.
  ecologicalSignificance: Premier "blue carbon" sinks that bury organic carbon up
    to 35 times faster than tropical rainforests. Their dense rhizome roots stabilize
    loose seabed sediments and nurture juvenile fish, sea turtles, and dugongs.
  interestingFact: Unlike algae or seaweeds, seagrasses have true roots, veins, underwater
    flowers, and fruit. They pollinate completely submerged using specialized waterborne
    pollen grains carried by gentle ocean currents!
  targetBiome: 1
  citationSource: United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) / Smithsonian Marine Station
  rdpReward: 75
  habitatPhoto: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 448e208ca3517e648953db4e00c34caa, type: 3}
